# Where this checkout's backend lives: the local PostgreSQL cluster, then the port the API
# serves on. The single source of all of them, read by the one thing that LAUNCHES a
# process rather than by any process itself: direnv, through `dotenv_if_exists` in .envrc.
# psql, createdb and initdb find the four PG* variables they already look for, and
# config.py composes the DSN from the same four names in the environment - it opens no
# file. Nothing in pixi.toml or pyproject.toml declares them a second time.
#
# 5432 is PostgreSQL's own default, so every client that falls back to it falls back to
# this cluster, and the loopback address keeps it off the network entirely. Both are baked
# into .pgdata/ by `pixi run backend-db-create`, so changing the port here means
# `pixi run backend-db-reset && pixi run backend-db-init` to rebuild the cluster on it.
# The one machine this does not suit is one already running a PostgreSQL of its own on
# 5432, where the two cannot both bind it; moving this cluster is the paragraph below.
#
# Committed, and holding development defaults only - the deployed API is handed a
# DATABASE_URL by whoever operates its database. To move the local cluster off a port
# something else has taken, edit PGPORT here and rebuild: a dotenv overwrites the ambient
# environment, so `export PGPORT=...` in a shell is not an override.
PGHOST=127.0.0.1
PGPORT=5432
PGUSER=expense_tracker
PGDATABASE=expense_tracker

# The port `poe dev` binds. uvicorn's CLI is a click command carrying
# auto_envvar_prefix="UVICORN", so uvicorn reads this name for --port itself, the way psql
# reads PGHOST - which is why no task passes --port. 8000 is uvicorn's own default, stated
# here so a second checkout moves its API off it exactly as it moves its cluster off 5432:
# by editing this file. Nothing bakes it into .pgdata/, so a change needs no reset.
UVICORN_PORT=8000

# The directory `poe load-expenses` reads: the `data/` directory inside a separate private
# repository, which is where that repository keeps one month file per bank and nothing
# else - its category vocabulary and decision maps sit in meta/, out of this glob.
# Real spending is confidential and this repository is public; nothing here clones the
# other one, updates it or tracks it, so keeping it current is your job. Absolute because it
# names a place outside this checkout, which means it is the one value in this file that
# is a fact about a machine rather than about the project. direnv expands $HOME when it
# loads this file, so the default below is a real path on any machine that keeps the
# clone where this one does; anywhere else, name it in backend/.env.local rather than
# editing this line - .envrc loads that file after this one, and it is gitignored.
#
# Unset and set-but-absent mean different things. Unset is a shell that never ran
# `direnv allow`, and the task refuses to run. Set but not on disk is simply a machine
# without the data repository, and loads nothing at all.
#
# The committed samples under backend/tests/data/expenses/ are the suite's fixtures, not a
# second source, so no task points here by default. To fill a database from them anyway:
# `cd backend && EXPENSE_DATA_DIR=tests/data/expenses poe load-expenses`.
EXPENSE_DATA_DIR=$HOME/projects/expense-data/data

# The rates directory the compose stack's load-currencies service mounts. A property of
# the backend's data rather than of the stack, which is why it is named here and not in
# stack/.env, and it is the one name in this file that no Python reads - `poe
# load-currencies` passes the path as an argument. Absolute because a bind source has to
# be: a relative one in a file loaded from `oci://` resolves against compose's download
# cache. direnv expands $PWD as THIS FILE'S directory when it loads it - measured, not
# assumed - so the path below is written relative to backend/ and arrives absolute.
CURRENCY_DATA_DIR=$PWD/data/currencies
